.\" whytime(1), hand-written. cargo xtask man adds the title line and
.\" replaces each ".\" @SECTION" line with that section rendered from cli.rs.
.SH NAME
whytime \- explain where a command's wall-clock time went
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY whytime
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG ...]
.YS
.SY whytime
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.BI \-p " PID"
.YS
.SY "whytime report"
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY "whytime doctor"
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.YS
.SY "whytime completions"
.I SHELL
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whytime
runs
.I COMMAND
with its arguments, traces it and every process it starts with eBPF, and prints
to standard error where its wall-clock time went: on a CPU, waiting for a CPU,
waiting on child processes, sleeping, in
.BR fsync (2),
reading files, waiting on network peers, pipes, locks and so on.
.I COMMAND
inherits standard input, output and error, so its own output passes through
unchanged, and
.B whytime
exits with
.IR COMMAND 's
exit status.
The report is printed when
.I COMMAND
exits.
.PP
The observation window starts when
.I COMMAND
is executed and ends when it exits (or when
.B \-\-duration
elapses).
Within it,
.B whytime
follows
.IR COMMAND 's
main thread and attributes every nanosecond to exactly one line of the report.
When the thread blocks on something another traced task provides (a child it
waits for, a pipe another process writes, a lock another thread releases), the
time is attributed to what that task was doing meanwhile: it follows the waker.
The result is one critical path through the process tree, and the shares of the
report lines sum to 100%.
.PP
With
.BI \-p " PID"
(attach mode),
.B whytime
observes a process that is already running, and its descendants, instead of
starting one; see
.BR "ATTACH MODE" .
.PP
.B whytime
needs the
.B CAP_BPF
and
.B CAP_PERFMON
capabilities (or root) to load its tracing programs; see
.BR PRIVILEGES .
.B whytime doctor
checks whether this system can run it and prints exact fixes; see
.BR whytime\-doctor (1).
.\" @OPTIONS
.\" @COMMANDS
.SH THE REPORT
A run of a small shell script (its exit status was 0):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ \fBsudo whytime \-\- sh \-c 'sleep 0.25;\fP
    \fBdd if=/dev/zero of=blob bs=1M count=64 conv=fsync status=none;\fP
    \fBxz \-T2 \-6 \-k \-f \-c blob | sha256sum >/dev/null;\fP
    \fBpython3 \-c "import time; time.sleep(0.1)"'\fP
wall 1.61s
cpu 1.85s across 5 processes \(pc 1.1\(mu parallel
  61%  waiting on child: xz
  16%  timer sleep (sleep)
   8%  waiting on child: dd
   6%  timer sleep (python3)
   5%  on\-CPU (python3)
   2%  fsync on /var/tmp/wt\-man
   2%  other
critical path: 5 processes \(pc 0 events lost
.EE
.RE
.PP
Line by line:
.TP
.B wall
The length of the observation window.
.TP
.B cpu
On-CPU time of every traced thread within the window, the number of processes
that had any, and their ratio to the wall time: how many CPUs were busy on
average.
This counts all the work, including work that ran in parallel with the critical
path and therefore appears in no line below.
.TP
.I share label
The report lines, largest first: every line of at least 1%, at most 8 of them.
Each is a class (see
.BR CLASSES )
and a subject within it: a process name, a directory, a network peer or a
system call.
Here the shell spent 61% of the window waiting for
.BR xz ,
which was computing.
A
.B runqueue
line where one task held the CPU for at least half of it ends with
.RI "\(lq\(em mostly " "comm share" \(rq,
for example
.RB \(lq "waiting for a CPU (cc1) \(em mostly chrome 61%" \(rq.
.TP
.B other
The lines below 1% (or beyond the eighth) summed, so that the shares add up to
100%.
.TP
.B critical path
How many processes contributed time to the lines, and how many records the
kernel side lost (lost time is reported as
.BR "other (untraced)" ).
.TP
.B note:
Caveats for this run, one per line: time before an attach is not covered,
tracing programs that could not be attached, network peers that stayed numeric,
a run ended by
.B \-\-duration
or a signal, lost records.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-timeline ,
a section before the
.B critical path
line divides the window into 10 equal slices and shows up to 3 lines of each
(class, subject and share of the slice, at least 1%):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
timeline (161ms slices):
  0.00\(en0.16s  sleep sleep 98% \(pc cpu sh 1%
  0.16\(en0.32s  sleep sleep 57% \(pc child dd 42%
  0.32\(en0.48s  child xz 39% \(pc child dd 35% \(pc fsync /var/tmp/wt\-man 18%
  0.48\(en0.64s  child xz 100%
\&...
  1.45\(en1.61s  sleep python3 62% \(pc cpu python3 35%
.EE
.RE
.PP
Process names in subjects are the
.I comm
of the process image the time belongs to.
When processes with the same name but different arguments are on the path,
each is named by the shortest suffix of its arguments that tells them apart
.RB ( "sort \-n" ,
.BR "make \[u2026] all" ),
or by
.IR comm [ pid ]
when the arguments do not.
Paths are shown as the blocked process saw them, with the home directory of
the invoking user abbreviated to
.BR \(ti .
Control characters in subjects and notes are printed as
.BI \ex HH .
.SH CLASSES
Each line of the report belongs to one class (the
.B class
field of the JSON report).
The label is how the human report prints a line; time blocked in a system call
is classified by the call and the file descriptor it waits on.
.TP
.B cpu
.RI "on-CPU (" comm )
.br
.IR COMMAND 's
own process (any of its threads) was running on a CPU.
.TP
.B child
.RI "waiting on child: " name
.br
.I COMMAND
(or a process it waited for) waited while a descendant process was running on
a CPU; the subject is the descendant that was computing.
.TP
.B runqueue
.RI "waiting for a CPU (" comm )
.br
Runnable but not running: preempted, or woken and not yet scheduled.
The JSON line lists in
.B held_by
who held the CPU meanwhile
.RB ( idle
for an idle CPU).
.TP
.B sleep
.RI "timer sleep (" comm )
.br
.BR nanosleep (2),
.BR clock_nanosleep (2),
reads of a timerfd.
.TP
.B fsync
.RI "fsync on " directory
.br
Waiting for data to become durable:
.BR fsync (2),
.BR fdatasync (2),
.BR sync_file_range (2),
.BR syncfs (2),
writes to files opened with
.B O_SYNC
or
.BR O_DSYNC .
.BR sync (2)
and
.BR msync (2)
name the system call instead
.RB ( "fsync in sync" ).
.TP
.B file
.RI "file I/O on " directory
.br
Other I/O on regular files and block devices, and path system calls such as
.BR openat (2)
and
.BR stat (2),
which are named by the call
.RB ( "file I/O in newfstatat" ).
.TP
.B lock
.RI "file lock wait on " directory
.br
.BR flock (2),
.BR fcntl (2)
with
.B F_SETLKW
or
.BR F_OFD_SETLKW .
.TP
.B aio
.RI "async I/O completion (" comm )
.br
.BR io_uring_enter (2),
.BR io_getevents (2),
.BR io_pgetevents (2).
.TP
.B net
.RI "blocked on network: " peer " (" n " connections)"
.br
Internet sockets.
The peer is
.IB host : port
when the traced processes looked the address up during the window through a
resolver
.B whytime
reads (DNS over port 53, or systemd-resolved's varlink socket), otherwise
.IR ip : port .
.TP
.B ipc
.RI "IPC wait (" comm )
.br
Pipes, FIFOs, UNIX sockets, eventfds, System V semaphores and message queues,
POSIX message queues.
A wait ended by a process outside the traced tree is named
.RI "woken by " comm .
.TP
.B futex
.RI "lock/futex wait (" comm )
.br
.BR futex (2)
waits: mutexes, condition variables, thread joins, when the thread that ended
the wait could not be followed.
.TP
.B poll
.RI "poll/epoll wait (" comm )
.br
.BR poll (2),
.BR select (2),
.BR epoll_wait (2)
when no single resource explains the wait; inotify and fanotify reads.
A wait whose ready descriptor was seen is classified as that descriptor would
be (a socket as
.B net
or
.BR ipc ,
a FIFO as
.BR ipc ,
a character device as
.BR tty ).
.TP
.B signal
.RI "waiting for a signal (" comm )
.br
.BR pause (2),
.BR rt_sigsuspend (2),
.BR rt_sigtimedwait (2),
signalfd reads.
.TP
.B tty
.RI "terminal/device I/O (" comm )
.br
Terminals and other character devices.
.TP
.B fault
.RI "page faults on " directory
.br
Blocked inside a page fault: reading a mapped file (the subject is its
directory) or
.BR "anonymous memory (swap)" .
This takes precedence over the system call the fault interrupted.
.TP
.B other
.RI "other (" what )
.br
Everything else:
.B in syscall
.I N
(a system call
.B whytime
does not classify),
.B 32\-bit syscall
(i386 or AArch32 programs),
.B outside syscalls (page faults, kernel locks)
(blocked outside any system call),
.BR "stopped (signal or tracer)" ,
.B blocked since attach, syscall unreadable
(attach mode without ptrace access), and
.B untraced
(time whose records the kernel side lost).
.PP
Blocks in
.BR futex ,
.B ipc
and
.B poll
waits that a traced thread ended,
.BR wait4 (2)
and
.BR waitid (2)
for a child, and a
.BR vfork (2)
or
.BR posix_spawn (3)
until the child executes, are not reported as waits: the time goes to what the
thread that ended the wait was doing, recursively.
A
.B futex
or
.B poll
wait that ended by timeout and was retried at once is joined to the retry that
a traced thread ended.
.SH ATTACH MODE
.B whytime \-p
.I PID
observes the running process
.I PID
and its descendants, including the processes they start after the attach.
The window starts once
.B whytime
has recorded the state of every thread from
.I /proc
and ends when the process exits, when
.B \-\-duration
elapses, or when
.B whytime
receives
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
or
.B SIGHUP
(Ctrl-C).
.B whytime
never signals the target, and exits 0 after the report.
.PP
Time before the attach is not covered.
Each thread's first interval is classified from what
.I /proc
showed at the attach (its state, its system call and file descriptor).
Without ptrace access to the target (for example with
.B kernel.yama.ptrace_scope
at 1 or more under file capabilities), a thread blocked in a system call is
reported as
.B other
for that first interval, and a note says so.
.PP
.B whytime
refuses a
.I PID
that does not exist, 1, itself, a thread that is not a thread group leader, a
zombie, and, without
root or
.BR CAP_SYS_PTRACE ,
a process whose real, effective or saved user or group ID is not the invoking
user's; it then prints why and exits 1.
.SH TRACE FILES
.B \-\-record
.I FILE
writes everything the report is computed from to
.I FILE
alongside the normal run (wrap or attach mode, with any other option).
.B whytime report
.I FILE
reads it back and prints the identical report, byte for byte, and writes the
identical
.BR \-\-json ,
.BR \-\-perfetto ,
.B \-\-flamegraph
and
.B \-\-folded
output (flamegraphs only if the recording captured stacks).
Replaying needs no privileges, no BPF and none of the traced processes, so a
trace file can be examined on another machine of the same architecture.
See
.BR whytime\-report (1).
.PP
A trace file describes what the traced processes did and can contain
sensitive data: the argument vectors of every traced process (which may include
secrets passed on the command line), file and directory paths, your home
directory, network peers, the hostnames the processes looked up (from the raw
resolver messages, stored verbatim), names of processes outside the tree that
woke a traced thread or held its CPU, symbolized call stacks with
.B \-\-flamegraph
or
.BR \-\-folded ,
and in attach mode what
.B whytime
read from
.IR /proc .
Review a trace file before sharing it.
.SH FLAMEGRAPHS AND PERFETTO
.B \-\-flamegraph
.I FILE
writes an SVG wall-clock flamegraph of the critical path: its width is the
window, and every attributed nanosecond is in exactly one stack.
Each stack starts with the report line's label, then the process name, the
thread name when it differs, then the call stack: user frames, then kernel
frames marked
.BR _[k] .
On-CPU time is split over the stacks sampled at 199 Hz in that stretch; blocked
time gets the stack captured when the block began; waiting for a CPU ends in
.BR [runqueue] .
Placeholders:
.B [no sample]
(no on-CPU sample within 5 ms),
.B [no stack]
(a thread blocked since before an attach),
.B [stack lost]
(the kernel could not store it),
.B [unknown]
(an address in no executable mapping),
.BI [ library ]
(a mapped file without a covering symbol),
.B [kernel]_[k]
(kernel addresses hidden by
.IR /proc/kallsyms ).
The SVG is self-contained: click a frame to zoom, Ctrl-F or Search to
highlight frames matching a regular expression.
.PP
.B \-\-folded
.I FILE
writes the same stacks in the folded format
.RI ( frame ; frame ;...\&
.IR count ,
one line per distinct stack, counts in microseconds) for
.BR flamegraph.pl ,
.B inferno
or
.BR speedscope .
.PP
Either option makes
.B whytime
capture stacks while tracing.
User stacks are unwound by frame pointers only: code built without them shows
truncated stacks or
.BR [unknown] .
Symbols come from the symbol tables
.RB ( .symtab ,
else
.BR .dynsym ),
without debug information, so there are no inlined frames.
Stack capture needs
.B kernel.perf_event_max_stack
of at least 127; when it cannot work,
.B whytime
exits 125 with the fix before running
.IR COMMAND .
.PP
.B \-\-perfetto
.I FILE
writes the window as Chrome Trace Event JSON, to open in
https://ui.perfetto.dev or chrome://tracing: a
.B critical path
track with one slice per attributed stretch (named like the report lines), one
track per traced thread with its on-CPU, runqueue and blocked stretches, and
instant events for forks, execs and exits.
Timestamps are microseconds from the start of the window.
.PP
Under
.BR sudo ,
all output files are created by the invoking user, so they belong to them and
their permissions decide where
.B whytime
may write.
A file that cannot be created stops
.B whytime
before anything runs (exit status 125).
.SH JSON OUTPUT
.B \-\-json
prints the report to standard error as one JSON object instead of the text.
Its
.B schema
field is
.BR whytime.report/1 .
The top-level fields are:
.TP
.B schema
.RB \(dq whytime.report/1 \(dq.
.TP
.B command
.I COMMAND
and its arguments (attach mode: the target's argument vector).
.TP
.B attached_pid
The target PID; present only in attach mode.
.TP
.B wall_ns
Length of the window in nanoseconds.
.TP
.B ended_by
.BR \(dqexit\(dq ,
.B \(dqduration\(dq
or
.B \(dqinterrupt\(dq
(attach mode only).
.TP
.B exit_code
.IR COMMAND 's
exit status (128 plus the signal number if a signal killed it), or null while
it is still running.
.TP
.B parallelism
.BR cpu_ns ,
.B processes
and
.BR factor :
the
.B cpu
line of the text report.
.TP
.B lines
The breakdown, largest first, every line (not only those of at least 1%):
.BR class ,
.B share
(a fraction of
.BR wall_ns ;
the shares sum to 1),
.BR ns ,
.BR subject ,
and where they apply
.B connections
and
.B addr
.RB ( net
lines) and
.B held_by
.RB ( runqueue
lines).
.TP
.B timeline
With
.B \-\-timeline
only: 10 slices of
.BR start_ns ,
.B end_ns
and their
.BR lines .
.TP
.B processes
Every process of the traced tree:
.BR pid ,
.BR ppid ,
.BR comm ,
.BR argv ,
.B cpu_ns
(on-CPU time over all its threads),
.BR exit_code ,
and
.B critical
(whether any line's time came from it).
.TP
.B quality
.B events
(records received),
.B dropped
(records lost in the kernel), and whether the optional probe sets were attached:
.BR syscalls ,
.BR readiness ,
.BR faults ,
.BR dns .
.TP
.B notes
The caveats, as strings.
.PP
The schema changes only additively: new optional fields, new classes, new
notes.
Removing or renaming a field, or changing its type or meaning, changes the
schema name.
Consumers should ignore unknown fields and treat an unknown class as
.BR other .
.SH PRIVILEGES
Loading the tracing programs needs
.B CAP_BPF
and
.BR CAP_PERFMON ,
or root.
There are two ways to run
.BR whytime :
.TP
.BI "sudo whytime \-\- " COMMAND
.B whytime
runs as root and runs
.I COMMAND
as the invoking user (from
.B SUDO_UID
and
.BR SUDO_GID ,
with the groups of
.BR SUDO_USER ),
with
.BR HOME ,
.B USER
and
.B LOGNAME
set back to that user's.
.TP
.BI "sudo setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon+ep " /path/to/whytime
Once; then run
.B whytime
without sudo.
.I COMMAND
does not inherit the capabilities.
File capabilities are ignored on file systems mounted
.B nosuid
(often
.IR /tmp ),
under
.BR no_new_privs ,
and they are lost whenever the file is replaced: run
.B setcap
again after every upgrade.
The distribution packages do not set them.
.PP
Optionally,
.B CAP_SYS_PTRACE
(for example
.BR "setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon,cap_sys_ptrace+ep" )
lets
.B whytime
resolve file paths of descendants running as another user, read the system
calls of an attach target's threads under Yama's
.BR ptrace_scope ,
and attach to other users' processes.
Kernel frames in flamegraphs need
.I /proc/kallsyms
addresses: root, or
.B cap_syslog
in the file capabilities, or
.B kernel.kptr_restrict=0
with
.B kernel.perf_event_paranoid
at most 1.
.PP
.B whytime
needs Linux 5.18 or newer with BTF
.RI ( /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux )
on x86_64 or aarch64.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WHYTIME_LOG
Log level of
.BR whytime 's
diagnostics on standard error:
.BR off ,
.BR error ,
.B warn
(the default),
.BR info ,
.B debug
or
.BR trace .
.B debug
prints the kernel verifier's log when a tracing program fails to load.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, the
.B whytime doctor
report and the start-up diagnostics are not colored (they are colored only on a
terminal).
.TP
.BR SUDO_UID ", " SUDO_GID ", " SUDO_USER
When
.B whytime
runs as root with these set to another user (as
.BR sudo (8)
sets them),
.I COMMAND
runs as that user and output files are created as that user.
.TP
.B HOME
The home directory abbreviated to
.B \(ti
in paths, when the password database has none for the user.
.PP
.I COMMAND
inherits
.BR whytime 's
environment.
.B WHYTIME_STACK_FLIP_MS
and
.B WHYTIME_STACK_MAP_ENTRIES
exist for
.BR whytime 's
own tests only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.I status
.IR COMMAND 's
exit status, or 128 plus the signal number if a signal killed it.
With
.BR \-\-duration ,
.B whytime
reports when the time is up, then waits for
.I COMMAND
and still exits with its status.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.
.TP
.B 125
.B whytime
could not start tracing (it prints the failing
.B whytime doctor
checks), or could not create an output file.
.TP
.B 126
.I COMMAND
was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
.I COMMAND
was not found.
.PP
In attach mode:
.B 0
after the report,
.B 1
if
.I PID
cannot be attached to, and
.B 125
as above.
.B whytime report
exits 0, or 2 if the trace file cannot be replayed;
.B whytime doctor
exits 0 if
.B whytime
can run, 3 if it cannot.
.PP
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGQUIT
from the terminal reach
.I COMMAND
while
.B whytime
ignores them and reports after
.I COMMAND
exits;
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGHUP
sent to
.B whytime
are forwarded to
.IR COMMAND .
.SH EXAMPLES
Explain a slow build:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo whytime \-\- make \-j8
.EE
.RE
.PP
Grant the capabilities once, then run without sudo:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon+ep "$(command \-v whytime)"
whytime \-\- ./run\-tests.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Observe a running service for 30 seconds:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo whytime \-\-duration 30s \e
    \-p "$(systemctl show \-p MainPID \-\-value nginx)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Report on the first 10 seconds of a long job (it keeps running, and
.B whytime
exits with its status when it ends):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo whytime \-\-duration 10s \-\- ./long\-job
.EE
.RE
.PP
Record once, then examine the trace without privileges: as a timeline, as JSON
(free of the command's own standard error), and in Perfetto:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo whytime \-\-record slow.wt \-\- cargo test
whytime report slow.wt \-\-timeline
whytime report slow.wt \-\-json 2>report.json
whytime report slow.wt \-\-perfetto slow.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Draw a flamegraph of the critical path, and keep the folded stacks:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo whytime \-\-flamegraph build.svg \-\-folded build.folded \-\- make
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the system first, for scripts as JSON:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
whytime doctor
whytime doctor \-\-json | jq \-r .verdict
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
One critical path.
Work that ran in parallel with it (other threads, children other than the one
that ended a wait) is in no line; the
.B cpu
line and
.B processes[].cpu_ns
count it.
Of N parallel workers that are joined, the report follows the one whose exit
ended the wait.
.IP \(bu 2
Following trusts the last waker: a futex wait is followed to the thread that
woke it, which is the critical path only if that thread was what the waiter
needed.
Retried waits are joined by timing alone.
.IP \(bu 2
Dependencies outside the traced tree (a daemon, a display server, a remote
service) appear only as the resource
.I COMMAND
blocked on
.RB ( net ,
.BR ipc ).
.IP \(bu 2
Network peers are named only from lookups the traced processes made during the
window through DNS on port 53 or systemd-resolved; lookups before the window,
from
.IR /etc/hosts ,
by a caching daemon, or over DNS-over-TLS/HTTPS leave them as
.IR ip : port .
.IP \(bu 2
File paths are captured when a system call first blocks and are shown relative
to the blocked process's mount namespace; paths longer than 256 bytes or 32
components are truncated.
.IP \(bu 2
System calls of 32-bit programs are reported as
.BR "other (32\-bit syscall)" .
.IP \(bu 2
A preempted stretch of a CPU-bound thread is credited to the task that held the
CPU during its longest preemption.
.IP \(bu 2
Attach mode does not see time before the attach, and classifies each thread's
first interval from
.IR /proc ,
which can race with the thread by microseconds.
.IP \(bu 2
Flamegraphs unwind user stacks by frame pointers only, give a whole block the
stack at its start, and show interpreters' C frames, not script functions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whytime\-report (1),
.BR whytime\-doctor (1),
.BR whytime\-completions (1),
.BR time (1),
.BR perf (1),
.BR strace (1),
.BR bpftrace (8),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR setcap (8)
.PP
Project page: https://github.com/Arinerron/whytime
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at https://github.com/Arinerron/whytime/issues,
with the output of
.B whytime doctor
and, if possible, a trace file recorded with
.B \-\-record
of a minimal reproduction (see
.B TRACE FILES
for what it contains).
